  "summary": "The cycle-based verdict approach has several gaps worth addressing. First, asking the synthesis agent to judge 'convergence' vs 'stall' is inherently subjective and unreliable — there's no concrete metric proposed for what constitutes meaningful progress between cycles, so the verdict risks being arbitrary or model-dependent. A synthesis agent optimized for helpfulness may always say 'progress' to avoid appearing unhelpful, leading to runaway cycles despite the max_cycles cap. Second, the proposal doesn't address cost proportionality: each additional cycle multiplies API calls across all configured round models, but there's no mechanism to evaluate whether the incremental refinement justifies the cost — a feature worth $2 of ideation shouldn't consume $20 of cycles. Third, the 'stall' detection assumes repetition equals deadlock, but genuine disagreement between agents with different architectures (e.g., MiniMax vs Claude) may represent valuable diversity of perspective rather than entrenchment — collapsing that into a 'stall' verdict loses information. Finally, the proposal wraps run_ideating() in an outer loop but doesn't address state management: if cycle 2 builds on cycle 1's synthesis, the synthesis itself becomes a biased starting point that anchors subsequent rounds, potentially creating artificial convergence rather than genuine consensus."
